WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.465
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.372 --> 00:00:08.039
JOEL CONKLING: My
name is Joel Conkling.

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:10.500
I'm a product manager
for Earth Engine.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.460
Earth Engine's the
petabyte-scale processing

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:17.070
platform for geospatial
data at Google,

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:18.880
used by a wide
range of industries

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.160
we've been developing
over the last 10 years.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.490
And I'm excited to tell
you a little bit about it

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.972
and show you a few demos
that will illustrate

00:00:25.972 --> 00:00:26.930
some of what it can do.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.140
So when we think
about Earth Engine,

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.070
we think about two things--

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:36.060
one is it's a
massive data catalog

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:40.110
with open data from satellite
imagery, and many others,

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:42.990
and their computation
platform where you can analyze

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:45.940
that data in a variety
of different ways

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.970
to meet your use cases.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:52.530
We see users from agriculture
industry, forestry industry,

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:55.798
oil and gas, natural resources,
and a wide variety of others.

00:00:55.798 --> 00:00:57.840
And we'll go through a
use case here in a second.

00:01:00.545 --> 00:01:02.920
And we'll do that through the
lens of an agricultural use

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:03.740
case.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.980
So imagine you're
working at a company.

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:08.470
You have a customer who
is a farmer in Arizona.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.780
He wants to know how his
agricultural fields are doing.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:13.350
Maybe he's farming
corn, wheat, soy--

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.140
something like that.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.290
And we're going to go
through a use case that shows

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:18.582
how that works in Earth Engine.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.710
I mentioned the Earth
Engine data catalog.

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:27.670
It's about 500 data sets of
geospatial data, 30 petabytes

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.860
and growing with a wide
range of data types,

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:34.180
from climate and weather
data, imagery, and radar data,

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:37.930
geophysical, socioeconomic, air
quality data, and many more.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:40.190
You can bring your own data
to our attention, as well.

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:41.890
So if you're doing
analysis where

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:46.450
you like open data and your
own data, that's easy to do.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:49.220
And what that does is it
lets you get started faster.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:52.125
So instead of figuring
out what data you need,

00:01:52.125 --> 00:01:54.000
where do you find it,
how do you download it,

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.455
how do you pre-process it--

00:01:55.455 --> 00:01:56.830
what you can do
with Earth Engine

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:58.990
is just identify that
data in the catalog

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:01.718
and start your work--
start your hypothesis.

00:02:01.718 --> 00:02:03.760
And so I'm going to go to
a demo right now, which

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.300
will show us a little
bit about how that works.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:11.260
So here with five
lines of code there

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:14.945
in the middle of
the screen, we've

00:02:14.945 --> 00:02:16.320
pulled some data
in for analysis.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.420
The first line
identifies the data set.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:23.290
The second line says, what
date do we want to look at?

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:25.900
Here, on the third line,
we're doing some visualization

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.330
parameters, adding
it to the map,

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:32.031
and telling it what
part of the map to show.

00:02:32.031 --> 00:02:34.150
I hit Run.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.290
And you can see this data set.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:37.600
This is Landsat 8.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.150
It's a satellite imagery program
run by the US government.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.450
It covers the globe
every 16 days.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:45.190
We've asked it for one day.

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:47.650
And you can see the
path of the satellite

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:48.989
over the course of that day.

00:02:48.989 --> 00:02:52.482
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.120
JOEL CONKLING: So we haven't
asked much of Earth Engine yet

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.480
in terms of computation.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.420
But when you do start
to do computation,

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:04.920
you often start in
the Code Editor--

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.350
what we call the Code Editor.

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:07.950
And here at the
top in the middle

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.890
is where you write your script.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:14.620
Right above that, there's a Run
button, which I used just now,

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:16.590
which gives you the
ability to run calculations

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:17.797
in Earth Engine.

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:20.130
And then you can look at your
results on top of the map,

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:21.868
either here at the bottom.

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:23.410
Or if you click
somewhere on the map,

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:25.500
you can see the results
in the top right.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:29.670
You also have access to all of
your scripts, all of your data,

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:32.270
and documentation about
how to use Earth Engine

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:34.650
in the top left panel there.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:37.240
In addition to the Code
Editor environment,

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:39.563
we also have programming APIs.

00:03:39.563 --> 00:03:40.980
You can use those
programming APIs

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.620
in Jupiter colab notebooks--

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:48.900
environments like that-- or pull
data into your GCP work flows

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:50.250
with APIs, as well.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:53.160
I want to go through a
series of additional demos

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.943
here where we'll start to answer
that question for the farmer,

00:03:55.943 --> 00:03:57.360
how are my fields
doing this year?

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:04.010
The first thing
I'll do is show you

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:09.048
what it looks like when you
pull in 16 days of data, which

00:04:09.048 --> 00:04:11.090
should give us pretty full
coverage of the United

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:12.470
States and the whole globe.

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.370
So that's given the
Landsat satellite

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:20.970
times to cover the entire globe.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.970
As someone who's working to
answer questions for a farmer,

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.630
you might care about a
longer time series than that.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:28.575
So I'll make this a
six-month time series.

00:04:31.757 --> 00:04:32.840
And you can hit Run there.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:36.470
And so, again, with
relatively few lines of code,

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.060
not that much work to do.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:43.070
You're pulling in data from
six months worth of Landsat.

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:46.610
Hundreds of gigabytes of
data is at your fingertips.

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:50.130
And the Landsat catalog itself
goes back 30 or 40 years.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:54.200
So you can go way farther
back in history of the map.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:57.810
So we've also got this
median value here,

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:00.050
which is basically looking
at, over six months,

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:03.630
you've got 12 images for
each part of the planet.

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:05.690
It's looking at the
median value for each

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:08.930
of the bands in that image.

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:11.570
It turns out that clouds
are highly reflective.

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:16.490
So, if you want to find
clouds, change median to max.

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:18.560
And once that comes
up, you'll see

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:21.170
that there are clouds almost
everywhere over the United

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:24.170
States at some point during
that six-month period.

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:26.503
A quick and easy way
to mask out clouds,

00:05:26.503 --> 00:05:27.920
which is a more
common thing to do

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:33.290
is you change that median
to a min and run that.

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:36.680
And you've got a decent
cloud-free mosaic of the United

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:39.230
States or anywhere in the globe.

00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:42.478
Again, pretty simple and
easy to do an Earth Engine,

00:05:42.478 --> 00:05:43.270
not that much work.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:49.188
In the agriculture
space, you care how well

00:05:49.188 --> 00:05:50.230
is your vegetation doing?

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:51.870
How healthy is your vegetation?

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:54.537
And one common way of doing that
is with a normalized difference

00:05:54.537 --> 00:05:57.035
vegetation index or NDVI.

00:05:57.035 --> 00:05:58.410
It's a pretty
simple calculation.

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:01.980
We start with the same
code that we had before

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:03.870
to bring that imagery in.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:09.820
Define a function at NDVI, use
existing Earth Engine operators

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:12.015
to do that calculation.

00:06:12.015 --> 00:06:15.510
It's one band minus
another divided by one

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:18.220
band plus another--

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:22.110
add the results to the image
that you're working on.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:25.020
And then here, you map
that across all the imagery

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:27.850
in your collection.

00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:31.958
Define the palette,
add it to the map.

00:06:31.958 --> 00:06:33.750
Pick what part of the
map you want to show.

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:36.360
And in this case, I'm going
to show part of Arizona

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:40.680
where there's some agricultural
fields and hit Run.

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:43.290
And that'll start
pulling in NDVI data

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:45.210
on top of some imagery data.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:54.690
So what that NDVI is showing,
as you get higher values that

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:56.430
goes to darker green.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:59.130
And those dark green
values register

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:02.820
for where areas for
those lots of vegetation.

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:06.360
You can also move the
transparency sliders

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:09.480
up and down to get a sense of
what's behind that imagery.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:11.760
You can toggle the
layers on and off.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:14.970
So a variety of things you can
do to sort of inspect the data.

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:19.273
With a few more lines of
code, we can do a lot more.

00:07:19.273 --> 00:07:21.690
And here, we're, again, starting
with that same basic code

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:24.580
that we had in the
previous example,

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:28.830
adding a little bit
of UI on top of that--

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:33.060
in this case, for adding a
map and a label on one side

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:35.280
and mapping a label
on the other side,

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:39.030
linking those two maps with
a function called linker

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:41.557
and adding a split
panel on top of that.

00:07:41.557 --> 00:07:43.140
So with a few more
lines of code, now,

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:44.700
you're able to go
into Earth Engine

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:50.190
and scroll back and forth and
see NDVI, and how that relates

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:52.590
to the imagery underneath.

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:54.600
So a pretty useful tool
for analyzing that data.

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:00.530
And again, as a farmer,
you might care a lot

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:03.630
about how that data
is changing over time.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:08.840
So, in this code, I've added
another panel at the bottom

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:18.260
with this code here and added
a series chart to that panel.

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:20.090
And so, when I click
on the map, that's

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:26.960
going to show me the NDVI over
time with that zero to one

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:28.905
range where NDVI is grounded.

00:08:28.905 --> 00:08:31.280
And you can see that, generally
speaking for this farmer,

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:34.520
overall, in 2018, the
NDVI looks pretty good.

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:36.650
In July 18, it looks
like there was probably

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:40.289
a cloud over the farm.

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:43.640
So that's why that's
showing us a low number.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:48.110
The last demo I have here is
going to run a comparison,

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:50.240
just adding another
time series chart,

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:56.550
running comparison
between 2017 and 2018.

00:08:56.550 --> 00:09:00.240
So, as that comes up,
we'll have NDVI for 2017

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:04.620
on the left-hand side, NDVI for
2018 on the right-hand side.

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:06.420
And we'll start
to see comparisons

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:08.220
between the two data sets.

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:10.395
And that'll start to
give us an answer of,

00:09:10.395 --> 00:09:11.520
how healthy are the fields?

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:15.130
How much vegetation is on those
fields one year versus another?

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:19.210
And so, again, clicking
on roughly that same spot

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:21.890
and looking at the
results, we can

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:23.720
see that the NDVI in
2018 at the bottom,

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:29.720
here, was higher for most of
the year than it was in 2017.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:32.320
So this is all using data
that's in the public catalog.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:35.300
You can bring your own
data into this, as well,

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:37.870
and using components
that are easily

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:40.000
available in Earth
Engine to let you inspect

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:43.400
that data in various ways.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:44.715
So going back into the slides--

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:50.100
so we've spent a lot of time--

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:51.690
a few minutes-- in
the Code Editor,

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:54.120
which is where we do
interactive analysis.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:58.320
We've started to get a
simple answer for one farmer.

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:00.300
If you want to do
that at scale, that's

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.940
where you start using Earth
Engine's batch capability.

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:05.030
So that allows you
to scale globally.

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:06.450
So you've got
thousands of farmers

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:09.250
that you're serving
around the world--

00:10:09.250 --> 00:10:12.180
lets you automate runs
and lets you basically

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:14.820
take advantage of Earth
Engine's parallel computing

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:16.140
architecture.

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:18.150
And on the right, here,
we've got an example

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:21.630
from not the agriculture
space, but from work

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:25.513
that we did with the European
JRC, Joint Research Centre,

00:10:25.513 --> 00:10:27.930
where they wanted to attract
surface water over the course

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:29.910
of 30 years where our
service water been

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:31.650
changing over 30 years--

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:34.460
ended up being 10 million
hours of computation.

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:36.750
We put 10,000 CPUs
against it, which

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:39.870
allowed the computation to
finish in less than two days.

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:42.030
So when do you really
want to run a big job,

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:45.270
Earth Engine can help with that.

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:48.060
And if you want to build Earth
Engine analysis into an app,

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:50.380
into a web page,
into a mobile app,

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:52.830
Earth Engine APIs
allow you to do that.

00:10:52.830 --> 00:10:57.180
It gives your users the ability
to customize their data,

00:10:57.180 --> 00:11:00.160
customize the answers
that they want

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:04.320
and basically get a lot of
value and power of Earth Engine

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:07.600
without writing code
editor code themselves.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:10.020
So that's another feature
where Earth Engine can

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:12.060
help you scale your
analysis, have greater

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:13.477
impact with your
analysis, help it

00:11:13.477 --> 00:11:15.180
reach more and
more of your users

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:18.123
in a way that makes sense.

00:11:18.123 --> 00:11:19.040
So it's my last slide.

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:20.730
If you are interested
in Earth Engine--

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:21.980
you think it might be able
to help you solve some

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:23.480
of your problems faster--

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:25.910
you can go to
earthengine.google.com,

00:11:25.910 --> 00:11:27.170
click Sign Up.

00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:29.090
And Earth Engine's
free to evaluate.

00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:31.190
And you can start
kicking the tires,

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:34.190
start using the Code Editor, the
APIs, or whatever makes sense

00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:36.950
to you, and take it from there.

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:37.803
That's all I have.

00:11:37.803 --> 00:11:39.470
Thank you very much
for your attention--

00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:40.512
appreciate you listening.

00:11:40.512 --> 00:11:43.040
And, yeah, thanks for coming.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:43.940
